  "body": "### What should we add?\r\n\r\n#### The Problem\r\n\r\nThe specific implementations of `SparseLabelOp.from_polynomial_tensor` for the various operator types could benefit from a more flexibel implementation of the `label_template` which is used as the translation map from the `PolynomialTensor` key to operator key.\r\nThe standard case can be seen in the `FermionicOp` [here](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-nature/blob/61b994fcbc379307f746bf1cdf1b849afd21abe8/qiskitn_nature/second_q/operators/fermionic_op.py#L261) which works well as intended since it correctly maps from a key of length `n` to `n` operators being applied.\r\nHowever, the `VibrationalOp` hard-codes a rather odd and confusing case [here](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-nature/blob/61b994fcbc379307f746bf1cdf1b849afd21abe8/qiskit_nature/second_q/operators/vibrational_op.py#L302) because of a poor design choice in the `VibrationalIntegrals` taken [here](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-nature/blob/61b994fcbc379307f746bf1cdf1b849afd21abe8/qiskit_nature/second_q/operators/vibrational_integrals.py#L67).\r\n\r\nIf a more flexibel solution to the `label_template` can be found, this would allow to generalize the fix the odd case of the `VibrationalIntegrals` and could allow other use cases where a key of length `n` does not lead to `n` operators. Examples of this are:\r\n- the `VibrationalIntegrals` where in essence a 3-dimensional index (mode, modal 1, modal 2) (which can be generalized further to 4D by (mode A, modal 1, mode B, modal 2) with the common case of `A == B`) leads to 2 operators (i.e. fewer operators than matrix indices)\r\n- the `SeniorityZeroTransformer` where we will encounter 2-body terms (i.e. 4 operators) fully stored in 2D matrices (i.e. more operators than matrix indices)\r\n- other low rank representations such as the work of #654 or potential future storage containers aware of symmetries in the 2-body integral space\r\n\r\n#### The Solution\r\n\r\nI see two possible solutions:\r\n1. storing the label templates for each key in the `PolynomialTensor`\r\n2. somehow attach the label template to the stored array\r\n\r\nThe former seems rather cumbersome to hand around in the stack and further complicates the computation and cross-checking when working with `PolynomialTensor` instances. The latter however seems more feasible and #1033 proposes an implementation for a first step in this direction.",
